      "id": 1,
      "text": "Jetersville was first called Perkinsonville but local lore says it was named for John \"Black Jack\" Jeter.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jetersville%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A Civil War marker on old US 360 notes that on April 5, 1865, Lee found Union forces across his retreat line at Jetersville.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jetersville%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s post office, one of Amelia County’s earliest, began operating as \"Jetersville\" around 1826.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jetersville%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Jetersville School, built 1923‑24, was one of several Rosenwald Schools serving African American children in the county.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jetersville%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In the late 1800s and early 1900s, mica mines near Jetersville produced valuable deposits before running out.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
